      "text": "Here is a comparison of current PostgreSQL hosting pricing across Neon, Supabase, and Amazon RDS (as of November 2026):\n\n| Provider | Pricing Model | Base / Starting Price | Additional Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Neon** | Serverless / Usage-based | $0 / month base (No monthly minimum) [1] | Launch tier uses usage-based billing [1]. |\n| **Supabase** | Hybrid (Tier + Usage) | Starts at $25 / month [2] | Pro plan covers base usage; additional resource consumption incurs extra fees [2]. |\n| **Amazon RDS** | Infrastructure-based | Pay-as-you-go (Varies) [3] | Charges depend directly on instance class, region, storage type/size, and bandwidth [3]. |\n\n### Summary of Differences\n* **Neon** provides serverless, consumption-only pricing with no fixed monthly base fee for its Launch plan [1].\n* **Supabase** charges a fixed $25/month base fee for its Pro plan, with extra charges applied if usage exceeds plan limits [2].\n* **Amazon RDS** relies on traditional infrastructure provisioning, where costs vary strictly by chosen hardware instance, storage configuration, region, and network traffic [3].\n\n**Data Gaps:** Exact per-GB storage/bandwidth rates, specific RDS hourly instance prices, and free-tier limits were not provided in the search evidence.",
