      "text": "The **CAP theorem** (also known as Brewer's theorem) states that a distributed data store can simultaneously provide at most two of the following three guarantees:\n\n1. **Consistency (C):** Every read receives the most recent write or an error. All nodes see the exact same data at the same time.\n2. **Availability (A):** Every non-failing node returns a non-error response to every request, though it does not guarantee the response contains the most recent write.\n3. **Partition Tolerance (P):** The system continues to operate despite network partitions (communication loss or dropped messages between nodes).\n\n### The Trade-off\nBecause network failures and delays are inevitable in real-world distributed systems, **Partition Tolerance (P) is non-negotiable**. Therefore, when a network partition occurs, a system must choose between:\n\n* **CP (Consistency / Partition Tolerance):** The system prioritizes data correctness over uptime. It rejects or delays requests on isolated nodes to prevent reading or writing stale data, sacrificing **Availability**.\n  * *Examples:* HBase, MongoDB (primary-node operations), Redis (in certain cluster configurations), traditional relational DBs with synchronous replication.\n* **AP (Availability / Partition Tolerance):** The system prioritizes uptime over absolute data freshness. Nodes continue accepting reads and writes independently during a partition, accepting that data may temporarily diverge or return stale results, sacrificing **Consistency**.\n  * *Examples:* Apache Cassandra, Amazon DynamoDB (eventually consistent settings), CouchDB.\n\n*Note:* A \"CA\" system (Consistency + Availability without Partition Tolerance) is only theoretically possible on a single node or a non-partitionable network, which does not exist in real-world distributed infrastructure.",
